{"summary":"Annales Veteris Testamenti, a Prima Mundi Origine Deducti ('Annals of the Old Testament, Deduced from the First Origins of the World') is a 1650 work by Archbishop James Ussher of Armagh, representing the most comprehensive biblical chronology ever attempted. Drawing on the Hebrew Bible, Septuagint, ancient Near Eastern sources, Josephus, and Scaligerian chronological methodology, Ussher calculated that Creation occurred at nightfall preceding Sunday, 23 October 4004 BCE — in the Julian calendar. The work covered every major Old Testament event from Creation through the death of Nebuchadnezzar, correlating each with a precise astronomical and civil date.

The Annales was not a fundamentalist polemic but a serious work of humanist scholarship. Ussher cross-referenced Ptolemy's canon of kings, Eusebius's Chronicle, the Syriac Peshitta, and Arabic astronomical tables alongside the Hebrew text. His methodology was rigorous by seventeenth-century standards — he made explicit astronomical calculations to identify the Julian dates corresponding to biblical events, and carefully weighted different manuscript traditions.

The work's extraordinary cultural impact came in 1701, when Bishop William Lloyd inserted Ussher's dates in the margins of a new edition of the Authorised King James Bible. This edition was reprinted for over 250 years, making 4004 BCE the de facto Protestant creation date. Scientists from Hutton to Darwin explicitly framed their deep-time discoveries as refutations of Ussher's timeline, making the Annales an unintended catalyst of the geological and biological revolutions.","causes":["In the 17th century, Archbishop James Ussher of Armagh sought to create a comprehensive biblical chronology.","Ussher drew on ancient sources, including the Hebrew Bible and Septuagint, to establish a detailed timeline of human history.","The work was influenced by the growing interest in biblical scholarship and historical chronology during the Scientific Revolution."],"effects":["Ussher's Annals had a lasting impact on biblical scholarship and historical chronology.","The work influenced subsequent biblical scholars and historians, shaping our understanding of ancient history.","The Annals of the Old Testament remain a valuable resource for historians and scholars seeking to understand the geography, politics, and culture of ancient civilizations."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"james-ussher","sourceName":"James Ussher","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"annales-veteris-testamenti","targetName":"Annales Veteris Testamenti","context":"As Archbishop of Armagh, Ussher defined the scope and methodology of his work, creating a comprehensive biblical chronology."},{"sourceSlug":"septuagint","sourceName":"Septuagint","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"annales-veteris-testimenti","targetName":"Annales Veteris Testamenti","context":"The Septuagint, a Greek translation of the Hebrew Bible, was a key source used by Ussher in establishing his timeline."},{"sourceSlug":"hebrew-bible","sourceName":"Hebrew Bible","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"annales-veteris-testimenti","targetName":"Annales Veteris Testamenti","context":"The Hebrew Bible was the primary source used by Ussher in creating his comprehensive biblical chronology."},{"sourceSlug":"armagh","sourceName":"Armagh","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"annales-veteris-testimenti","targetName":"Annales Veteris Testamenti","context":"Armagh, Ireland, was the location of Archbishop Ussher's work and the site of his appointment as Archbishop."},{"sourceSlug":"scientific-revolution","sourceName":"Scientific Revolution","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"annales-veteris-testimenti","targetName":"Annales Veteris Testamenti","context":"The Scientific Revolution, a period of significant scientific and intellectual change, occurred during the time Ussher was working on his Annals."},{"sourceSlug":"biblical-scholarship","sourceName":"Biblical Scholarship","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"annales-veteris-testimenti","targetName":"Annales Veteris Testamenti","context":"Biblical scholarship and historical chronology were influenced by Ussher's work, which became a model for future biblical scholars."},{"sourceSlug":"historical-chronology","sourceName":"Historical Chronology","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"annales-veteris-testimenti","targetName":"Annales Veteris Testamenti","context":"Historical chronology was influenced by Ussher's work, which provided a detailed timeline of human history."}],"texts":[{"slug":"annalium-pars-posterior","name":"Annalium Pars Posterior","year":"1654","role":"Ussher's sequel continuing the timeline from Solomon to the New Testament"},{"slug":"king-james-bible","name":"King James Bible (1701 edition)","year":"1701","role":"The 1701 edition which first printed Ussher's dates in the margins"}],"evidence":[{"tier":"A","citation":"Ussher, James. Annales Veteris Testamenti. London: Flesher & Robinson, 1650. The complete primary text."},{"tier":"B","citation":"Barr, James. 'Why the World Was Created in 4004 BC.' Bulletin of the John Rylands Library 67 (1985): 575–608."},{"tier":"B","citation":"Gould, Stephen Jay. 'Fall in the House of Ussher.' Natural History, November 1991."}],"timeline":[{"year":"1650","event":"Published in London by Flesher and Robinson; immediately recognised as the definitive biblical chronology"},{"year":"1654","event":"Sequel Annalium Pars Posterior published, completing the timeline through the New Testament"},{"year":"1701","event":"Bishop William Lloyd inserts Ussher's dates in the margins of the Authorised KJV Bible"},{"year":"1859","event":"Darwin's On the Origin of Species explicitly references Ussher's timescale as the framework being overturned"}],"places":[{"name":"Armagh","role":"Birthplace"}],"quote":"","externalLinks":[],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_THOUGHT","INTELLECTUAL_HISTORY","PRINT_CULTURE_AND_KNOWLEDGE","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"sessionId":"vscode-batch-67-may2026","enrichedBy":"claude-sonnet-4.6·cloud·GH#vscode","enrichedAt":"2026-05-17T04:19:49.668663+00:00","_editLog":[],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Ussher's Annals had a lasting impact on biblical scholarship and historical chronology, shaping our understanding of ancient history.","significanceCategory":"regional"}}
